   "source": [
    "## Matriz de Covarianza\n",
    "\n",
    "> La covarianza mide cómo dos variables cambian juntas. La matriz de covarianza generaliza esto para múltiples variables."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "covariance-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"=== Matriz de Covarianza ===\")\n",
    "\n",
    "datos = np.array([[2, 4], [4, 6], [6, 9], [8, 12]])\n",
    "print(\"Datos (2 variables):\")\n",
    "print(datos)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nMatriz de covarianza:\")\n",
    "print(np.cov(datos.T))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nExplicación:\")\n",
    "print(\"  [0,0] = varianza de variable 1\")\n",
    "print(\"  [1,1] = varianza de variable 2\")\n",
    "print(\"  [0,1] = [1,0] = covarianza entre var 1 y var 2\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "correlation",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Correlación de Pearson\n",
    "\n",
    "> El coeficiente de correlación de Pearson mide la relación lineal entre dos variables, con valores entre -1 y 1."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "id": "correlation-code",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "print(\"=== Correlación de Pearson ===\")\n",
    "\n",
    "x = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n",
    "y = np.array([2, 4, 5, 4, 5])\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"x: {x}\")\n",
    "print(f\"y: {y}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "correlation = np.corrcoef(x, y)[0, 1]\n",
    "print(f\"\\nCorrelación de Pearson: {correlation:.4f}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"\\nInterpretación:\")\n",
    "print(\"  1.0  = correlación positiva perfecta\")\n",
    "print(\"  0.5  = correlación positiva moderada\")\n",
    "print(\"  0.0  = sin correlación\")\n",
    "print(\"  -0.5 = correlación negativa moderada\")\n",
    "print(\"  -1.0 = correlación negativa perfecta\")"
   ]
  },
